; e106_sockvec_accept.chiral — E106 conformance: THE ACCEPT (checker leg).
;
; Holds ≥2 move-only `Sock` caps in a `SockVec`, exercises `sv-count` (the
; discard-free `length`) and `sv-detach-at` (the swap-remove analog), then
; drains every remaining cap exactly once. It PASSES the linear checker — the
; `1`-fielded container threads move-only, no cap is dropped or duplicated.
; (Linear values are threaded through `case`/application, never `let`-bound —
; a `let` binder is unrestricted quantity, so a linear value there is rejected.)
;
; It does NOT run natively: draining consumes each cap via `sock-close`, which
; is a `porttype` close absent from the lowering table (`crossing-wraps.chiral`),
; so B1 accepts it at the type level but emits no native entry
; (`no emitted label for entry compile-main`). That is decision 2 / the E107
; follow-on, NOT E106. The exactly-once guarantee is proved HERE, at the checker.
(import "prelude/prelude")
(import "ports/ports")
(import "capability/lincoll")

(def compile-main (=> I64 I64)
  (lam (n)
    (case (sock-connect "a")
      ((conn-err e) 1)
      ((conn-r s0)
        (case (sock-connect "b")
          ((conn-err e) (case (sock-close s0) (unit 2)))  ; s0 still live: close it
          ((conn-r s1)
            ; hold two caps [s1, s0]; count without discarding (threads the vec
            ; back), then detach the head, close it, and drain the rest — each
            ; cap consumed exactly once.
            (case (sv-count (sv-push s1 (sv-push s0 sv-nil)))
              ((count-r cnt v2)
                (case (sv-detach-at v2 0)
                  ((det-none v3)   (case (sv-drain v3) (unit cnt)))
                  ((det-r s rest)
                    (case (sock-close s)
                      (unit (case (sv-drain rest) (unit cnt))))))))))))))
